  "body": "Time-varying Gaussian-dyadic helper gauges satisfy\n`C_G >= (C_0 + C_1)/2 + 2M`, where M counts every odd-parity switch,\nincluding entry and return. One common rounding bit is used across helpers\nand time. Under the specified shared-product ADD lowering and separately paid\ntwo-real-register transitions, the scalar-cost infimum equals that of positive\nreal-dyadic temporal gauges.\n\n**Status: scoped negative result with a sharp finite fixture; no new kappa.**\nThis extends Hanson Sun's PR1490 static common-rounding argument to temporal\nschedules. It covers every finite schedule, all integer exponents and all unit\nphases by a written proof. The finite checks support that proof and do not\nconstitute a formal multiplication theorem.\n\nThe seed-selected k8 pair L225/L340 attains an eight-primitive penalty in both\norientations. Its complete Gaussian invoices are 148,532 forward and 148,556\nbackward; either common real rounding costs 148,524 and 148,548. All dirty\nhelpers restore and the paid profile stays unchanged. A separate mixed-parity\nfixture forces a Gaussian coefficient at the shared ADD and passes the full\ncolumn replay. No joint-pair optimum or scalar record is claimed.\n\nOnly `research/temporal-gaussian-rounding-seed22/` is added. Review the temporal\ndeterminant/mixing argument and checkpoint contract in README.md. Original\nSCALEs remain separately charged; fused SCALEs, joint helper transitions,\ncross-macro product sharing and precision/admission closure are outside the\ntheorem.\n\nReproduce with standard-library Python 3.12:\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -B scripts/research_package.py --package research/temporal-gaussian-rounding-seed22\n```\n\nExpected: `PASS_TEMPORAL_GAUSSIAN_ROUNDING_NO_NEW_KAPPA`,\n`PASS_TEMPORAL_GAUSSIAN_EXPORT read_only=1 corruptions=3 optimized=1`, and\n`PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE`.\n\nValidation passes: 3,136 phased transitions; 36,608 ADD inequalities and 1,504\nliteral six-column macros; 864 exhaustive tiny planner paths; four bounded\nhelper/orientation dynamic programs; seven complete 712-column complex\nfixtures per orientation; 11 mathematical/source controls and 3 actual file\ncorruptions. Registered Python 3.12.15 and optimized read-only relocated\nPython 3.11.15 agree. All 25 selected-result tests pass after the upstream\nrefresh. Full selected cold replay, native hosting, Linux CI and human review\nare not claimed.\n\nResearch base: `ef5f39403610d231a0c27e8a6b4f08c10209bcd6`.\nIntegration base: `c5108f0f68d482e5712a8e26a5978d47762dfc16`.\nBoth live inputs, the selected record and applicable rules are unchanged.\nOS assignment seed 22 selects the complex-supplier track. TASK.json records\nthree candidates and the explicit change from the paid-inventory catalogue\ncard to this permitted scalar-circuit scope. No pinned sprint issue exists,\nso coordination is local. The complete refresh covered 715 open PRs and 217\ndiscussion records, followed by a check of all 13 updated PRs since 02:54 UTC.\n\nCredit Hanson Sun/@bbMisk PR1490 at\n`db410cbd344bcf58f754689ae893d4c31a01f32d`; Sagar Thakrar PR696/1259/1380;\nwoklerx PR1161; Hanson PR1214; Philipp PR1263/1310/1322; Cinna Davis PR1388;\nand the retained Jeremy Kothe, Rohan Arun, Hao Yan/icekylinx and PR589/555\nsupplier lineage. Exact source pins and scope boundaries are in SOURCES.json.\nNew work is Apache-2.0. Prepared by Sagar Thakrar with substantial OpenAI\nCodex assistance in proof, code, verification and prose. One agent; no\nindependent human review.\n",
